  "text": "Question eight. When should you be careful with DISTINCT? Three orders, joined to their items, give five rows. Too many. So someone adds DISTINCT, and the three orders look right again. But sum of distinct amount gives one fifty. Orders two and three both cost fifty, and DISTINCT kept that amount once. The real total is two hundred. Before you reach for DISTINCT, find out why the duplicates are there. And the tool that fixes most of these is naming a step.\n\nQuestion nine. A C T E or a subquery? A C T E is a named step, written once at the top, with WITH. Then you can use it as often as you like. Question ten. What's a correlated subquery? One that reads the outer row. Take orders above their own customer's average. The subquery runs again for every order. Ava averages eighty, Ben fifty-five, Cy fifty. Three orders beat their own customer's average. An uncorrelated subquery runs once. Against everyone's average, about sixty-two, only two orders do. And a C T E gets the same three by working out each average once, then joining. Easier to read, and easier to check.",
